Chapter 13 — Part 1
Black smoke erupted from the crater.
It moved like it had weight—thick, coiling tendrils that snapped around Nomu’s limbs and torso before the monster could throw another punch. The thing was yanked backward, yellow eyes widening as the smoke constricted. Regeneration fought it. The smoke didn’t care.
Curtis floated up out of the broken ground.
The mask was whole again. Antlers complete. Eye slits lit—red and blue, steady. The armor had reformed, but the green lines were gone. In their place the plates looked darker, almost wet, edges trailing the same black smoke that held Nomu. The cloak hung heavier. Something in the way he rose didn’t match the Curtis who had been taking the beating seconds ago.
He hung a meter above the crater, boots not touching anything, smoke curling off his shoulders. The class stared. Aizawa stopped mid-step.
Something was wrong.
The posture was too still. The head tilt too precise. When Curtis spoke, the filter carried a lower, smoother edge that hadn’t been there before.
“Stay down.”
The smoke tightened around Nomu. The monster thrashed. Curtis didn’t blink. The red eye burned hotter than the blue. On the stairs, Midoriya’s voice came out small.
“…Curtis?”
No answer. The smoke kept coiling. The repaired mask faced the plaza like it was seeing the fight for the first time.
Curtis hung above the crater, smoke still coiling off him. His voice came quiet, almost gentle, through the repaired mask.
“Please cover your eyes. I can’t hold back anymore.”
The armor changed.
Black plates drank the light. Red fissures split open across the chest, shoulders, and gauntlets—glowing like fresh wounds. The antlers darkened to the same matte black. The cloak shredded at the edges and reformed heavier, trailing shadow. Murderous intent poured off him in waves. Malice. Something old and cold that made the air taste like blood. Fear rolled outward with it, thick enough that the nearest students staggered.
Mina dropped. Kirishima’s hardening cracked under the pressure. Bakugo’s explosions died in his hands. Midoriya’s knees hit the step. Uraraka whimpered and clamped both hands over her eyes. Even Todoroki flinched, ice spiderwebbing uncontrolled across the walkway.
Aizawa’s capture weapon fell slack. His eye watered from the sheer output of killing intent. “Beardy—don’t—”
Nomu thrashed in the black smoke and couldn’t break free. Shigaraki, still half-buried in the far wall, went still for the first time. Kurogiri’s mist recoiled.
Curtis’s head tilted. The red eye burned hotter than the blue. The fear kept leaking. The malice didn’t fade.
“I warned you.”
Curtis moved.
One strike. His fist met Nomu’s chest and the shockwave detonated upward. The monster left the ground like it had been fired from a cannon. It smashed through the dome’s ceiling in a burst of shattered paneling and daylight, black smoke still clinging to its limbs as it vanished into open sky.
Curtis dashed after it. A blur of black and red armor, cloak streaming, gone through the same hole before the debris finished falling.
Down in the plaza the silence was absolute.
Shigaraki pushed himself halfway out of the rubble, neck still scratched raw, red eyes wide. “That… wasn’t the same person.” His voice came out thin. “The output. The intent. Kurogiri—”
Kurogiri’s mist form had pulled tight, yellow eyes narrowed. “The signature changed. This is no longer a student exercising restraint. We should withdraw.”
“Withdraw?” Shigaraki laughed once, unsteady. “All Might isn’t even here and the faculty pet just put our Nomu through the roof. What the hell did U.A. enroll?”
Above them, through the ragged hole in the dome, black-red lightning flickered against the sky. The fear still lingered in the plaza. No one on the ground moved to follow.
They came down together.
Nomu hit the plaza first, the impact cratering what was left of the floor. Curtis landed on its chest a half-second later, black-red armor still trailing smoke. He didn’t punch this time.
His hands dug in. Armored fingers tore through the toughened hide and ripped slabs of flesh free. Regeneration tried to knit. He didn’t give it the chance. He grabbed exposed bone—ribs, forearm, clavicle—and snapped them one after another. The cracks were sharp, final. Shock absorption meant nothing against something that simply removed the parts.
“Can’t shock absorb this.” His voice stayed low, almost conversational under the mask. Another rib broke in his grip. “And your skin’s tough. But I’ve ripped apart tougher materials in my long, ancient time.”
Nomu thrashed. The black smoke pinned its limbs. Curtis kept tearing. Flesh. Bone. The wet sound carried. Students who hadn’t covered their eyes yet did now. Mina was sobbing. Midoriya had gone pale. Even Bakugo looked shaken.
Shigaraki stared from the rubble, fingers frozen. Kurogiri’s mist had gone completely still.
Curtis snapped another bone, red eye burning, and didn’t slow down. The Nomu’s regeneration was losing. The plaza floor ran dark under them.
Shigaraki and Kurogiri moved at the same time.
The ringleader burst from the rubble, hand outstretched, Decay already active. Kurogiri’s mist surged to flank, warping the space around Curtis to cut off escape. They closed from both sides while Nomu still thrashed under him.
Curtis didn’t turn. He snapped another bone, then Blinked clear of a Decay swipe and drove an elbow into Kurogiri’s mist core hard enough to disrupt it. Shigaraki’s fingers grazed his cloak—the ancient material resisted, but the edge still crumbled. The three-way clash exploded across the plaza.
The class jumped in.
Bakugo’s explosions roared as he launched from the walkway, aiming for Kurogiri. Kirishima hardened and charged Shigaraki’s blind side. Todoroki’s ice erupted across the floor, trying to pin feet. Midoriya moved despite Aizawa’s earlier order, green sparks flickering as he closed on the ringleader. Uraraka floated debris into Kurogiri’s path. Mina’s acid splashed at Shigaraki’s coat. The plaza turned into overlapping quirks and shouting.
Curtis stayed on Nomu, ripping and breaking, black smoke lashing out to swat anyone who got too close to his target. Shigaraki snarled and decayed a wave of ice. Kurogiri warped Bakugo’s blast away. The students pressed anyway.
Then the front of the dome exploded inward.
All Might stood in the new breach, costume bright, smile gone, eyes locked on the battlefield. Dust and daylight poured around him.
“I AM HERE.”
The fighting stuttered. Even Nomu’s thrashing paused. All Might’s gaze swept the ruined plaza—Aizawa down, Thirteen down, students bloodied, Curtis in the black-red armor still tearing into the monster—and the smile didn’t return.
“Everyone,” he said, voice low and carrying, “step back.”
He moved.
